# THIS IMPLEMENTATION'S. The platform root has its own for the things every
# implementation would share (`__pycache__/`, `.venv`, `.env`); everything here
# is anchored to a directory that only this implementation has, which is
# precisely what cannot be written at the root -- a pattern containing a slash
# is relative to the .gitignore file it appears in, so `workspace/*` up there
# would never match `impl/claude-python/workspace/*`.

# Agent sandbox
# NOTE: "workspace/*" not "workspace/" -- git never descends into a directory
# excluded by the directory form to evaluate a negation, so "!workspace/.gitkeep"
# below would silently never take effect (confirmed with `git check-ignore -v`
# during Task 11 verification).
workspace/*
!workspace/.gitkeep

# Scratch space -- every temporary/throwaway directory belongs under here.
# Same "temp/*" + negation form as the workspace block above; see that NOTE.
temp/*
!temp/.gitkeep

# NOTHING under `schema/` is ignored. Every file there is written by
# scripts/dump-schema.py, carries the version it was generated from in its name,
# and is read by code -- so it is a versioned artifact, not scratch. Decision
# 2026-08-06; this block previously ignored the directory and is kept only to say
# why the rule reversed.
#
# What it used to say: these are derived, `alembic upgrade` and `/openapi.json`
# are the sources of truth, and a stale committed copy is worse than none. It
# offered its own escape hatch -- "if you would rather COMMIT them, delete this
# block" -- and that is what happened.
#
# What it cost while it stood. The pattern was written `schema/`, UNANCHORED, so
# it matched a directory of that name at EVERY depth, including `docs/schema/`
# where the published OpenAPI documents lived. Measured 2026-08-06:
# `git log --all -- '*openapi-*.json'` returned NOTHING. No released version's
# document had ever been committed. AS-24 of the signed interface contract
# promises every one of them is published, ST-14 has a consumer pin it, and R-20
# settles ownership disputes against it -- and a fresh clone had none of them,
# while both sides' conformance suites passed because each compared the served
# document against a local file that happened to be sitting there.
#
# Staleness is the real objection to committing derived output, and the answer
# to it is a test rather than an ignore rule: tests/test_api_meta.py pins the
# OpenAPI document against what the app serves, and tests/test_migrations.py
# pins the SQL against what the migrations render.
